  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nMoist surface conditions due to yesterday's rain, and light\nsurface winds with weak high pressure overhead will result favor\nfog formation this morning. Patchy fog is possible across\nportions of the forecast area, especially for our southwest\ncounties (e.g. Hancock to Knox Counties) where there could be\ngreater coverage of fog.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe stationary front that has been impacting the broader region\nhas sagged south towards the lower Ohio Valley. A series of\nmid-level shortwaves are expected to traverse the broader\nnorthwest flow, with additional rounds of convection expected.\nThe latest high resolution models have thunderstorms with heavy\nrain displaced to our southwest, with mainly rain showers\naffecting parts of northern Ohio tonight into Friday. This is\ngenerally west of I-77 with our southwest counties (Hancock to\nKnox Counties) seeing the best shot at rain showers. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... \nLow pressure moves eastward across the Great Lakes region, \nlifting a warm front across the area Saturday night. The entire \nforecast area will be within the warm sector on Sunday, with \nvery humid conditions expected. A couple rounds of thunderstorms\nare expected with this system.\n\nThe first round of thunderstorms is expected with the warm front\nSaturday night, generally moving west to east. This is most\nlikely to occur overnight, though a few models do linger this\nactivity into the morning ours, depending on the speed in which\nthe warm front lifts across the area. There is a low risk for\nsevere weather.\n\nAdditional convective development is expected Sunday afternoon \nand evening. High instability along with modest mid-level shear \nmay produced an organized severe weather threat. As always, \nthere is some uncertainty with timing/location of greatest \nsevere weather threat but this time frame is worth keeping an \neye on.\n\nThe cold front sweeps through Sunday night with high pressure\nbuilding in on Monday and Tuesday.",
